      "id": "test-everest-trek",
      "title": "Mera Peak Climbing and Amphu Lapcha Pass - 19 Days",
      "slug": "test-everest-base-camp-trek",
      "shortDescription": "Mera Peak Climbing and Amphu Lapcha Pass Expedition is a challenging high-altitude adventure that combines trekking, peak climbing, and crossing a technical mountain pass. It takes you through the quiet Hinku and remote Hongu Valleys, far from the busy Everest routes. You’ll climb Mera Peak (6461m)—the highest trekking peak in Nepal offering amazing views of Everest, Lhotse, Makalu, Cho Oyu, and Kangchenjunga. After the summit, the journey continues into the wild and untouched Hongu Valley, ending with the thrilling Amphu Lapcha Pass (5845m), which requires technical gear and professional guided support.\n\nMera peak climbing with Amphu Lapcha trek is only for experienced trekkers or those with basic mountaineering skills who are ready to experience true Himalayan expedition. Along the way, you’ll see glaciers, alpine lakes, and massive peaks, and experience the culture of remote Sherpa villages. This is not just a trek, it’s a remote, raw, and rewarding journey through some of the most stunning and less-traveled parts of the Himalayas.",
      "description": "Mera Peak Climbing and Amphu Lapcha Pass Expedition is a challenging high-altitude adventure that combines trekking, peak climbing, and crossing a technical mountain pass. It takes you through the quiet Hinku and remote Hongu Valleys, far from the busy Everest routes. You’ll climb Mera Peak (6461m)—the highest trekking peak in Nepal offering amazing views of Everest, Lhotse, Makalu, Cho Oyu, and Kangchenjunga. After the summit, the journey continues into the wild and untouched Hongu Valley, ending with the thrilling Amphu Lapcha Pass (5845m), which requires technical gear and professional guided support.\n\nMera peak climbing with Amphu Lapcha trek is only for experienced trekkers or those with basic mountaineering skills who are ready to experience true Himalayan expedition. Along the way, you’ll see glaciers, alpine lakes, and massive peaks, and experience the culture of remote Sherpa villages. This is not just a trek, it’s a remote, raw, and rewarding journey through some of the most stunning and less-traveled parts of the Himalayas.",
